# Normalize line endings so a Windows checkout doesn't show phantom
# whole-file diffs. Without this, `core.autocrlf=false` (common on Windows dev
# machines) makes git byte-compare a CRLF worktree against LF index blobs, and
# every affected file reads as fully modified. That state was latent across 278
# tracked files here — most stayed hidden only because git's stat cache had not
# re-read them.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary per file, so the 26 binary blobs
# (PNGs, JPGs, and daemon/ios/service-clients.ts) keep their exact bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# scripts/install.ps1 used to carry `-text` here, exempting it from the rule
# above. The reason was that its blob was committed with CRLF and `-text` ("no
# conversion in either direction") kept those bytes untouched. That reason is
# gone: the 0.23.18 upstream merge replaced the file with upstream's production
# installer lane, whose blob is LF, so there is no CRLF blob left to preserve and
# the exemption now only weakens things — under `-text` a CRLF-saving editor
# would commit CRLF, while `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes it. The delicate part of
# this file was never its line endings anyway: it is BOM-less UTF-8 with
# non-ASCII content, and the `text` attribute does not touch bytes other than
# EOLs. Dropping the exemption is a no-op on the current tree (blob and worktree
# are already LF) and it stops a future upstream merge of this file from
# conflicting on line endings alone.
